window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][44] = "<ol>\n<li><strong>Define the task and its failure types</strong> with examples, including what the feature must decline.</li>\n<li><strong>Collect cases</strong> from real or realistic inputs, stratified by slice; add adversarial and unanswerable cases; remove duplicates; keep a held-out portion that engineers do not tune against.</li>\n<li><strong>Write the rubric</strong> with two or three criteria and scored examples; pilot it on 30 cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Label</strong> with two independent reviewers on a sample; compute agreement; revise the rubric until agreement is acceptable; resolve disagreements.</li>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> score the current approach (and a simple alternative) to learn where you start.</li>\n<li><strong>Set the pass bar</strong> from the cost of error, with per-slice minimums and zero tolerance for the worst failures.</li>\n<li><strong>Automate:</strong> run on every change; report overall and per slice with intervals.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitor in production:</strong> sample live traffic for review, track user signals, and add every found failure to the &quot;never again&quot; set.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Beware overfitting to the eval.</strong> If engineers tune prompts and models against the same cases repeatedly, scores rise without real improvement (C23.2). Keep a hidden test set, refresh it from live traffic, and watch for a gap between eval and production performance.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-c25-3-ai-metrics-and-online-experimentation\"></span>\n<h4>C25.3 AI metrics and online experimentation</h4>\n<p>C23.7 gave a metric stack for ranked systems; for AI features use the same layers (FW-C25-03):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Danger if alone</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Output quality (eval)</td>\n<td>Faithfulness, helpfulness, error rate by category, appropriate refusals</td>\n<td>Measures the model, not the product</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Product behavior</td>\n<td>Task success, edits to the output, acceptance, escalation to a person, time to complete</td>\n<td>Acceptance can reward over-reliance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business</td>\n<td>Retention, conversion, deflected support contacts, revenue per user</td>\n<td>Slow; confounded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost and speed</td>\n<td>Cost per task, 95th-percentile latency</td>\n<td>Cheap and fast can be wrong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safety and trust</td>\n<td>Reports, harmful-output rate, complaints, opt-outs</td>\n<td>Rare events need large samples</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The acceptance trap.</strong> A high acceptance rate for suggestions can mean the feature is good or that users trust it too much. Measure <em>accepted and wrong</em>: sample accepted outputs for errors, and track later corrections and complaints. Worked example 3 shows that at an 8% error rate among accepted suggestions the feature costs more time than it saves, and that the break-even error rate is about 3.7%.</p>\n<p><strong>Experiments on AI features (C14, C17).</strong> Outputs vary run to run, so measure at the level of the user or task over many cases, not single examples. Reading effects by week matters because novelty draws early use and users adapt (they learn to trust, to check, or to stop using it) (C14.4). Use a holdout for long-term effects. Offline evals gate; online tests judge. Run <strong>shadow mode</strong>: a deployment in which the new system runs on live inputs and its outputs are logged and judged but not shown to users, so you can measure quality on real traffic without risk. Then a canary to a small group (C17.2).</p>\n<p><strong>Guardrail metrics.</strong> Safety, cost and latency must not worsen beyond margins, as in C14.5, with extra care for rare harms that need large samples: to see a harm occurring at 1 in 10,000 you need on the order of tens of thousands of cases per arm.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-c25-4-safety-guardrails-abuse-designing-for-failure\"></span>\n<h4>C25.4 Safety, guardrails, abuse; designing for failure</h4>\n<p>Safety for AI products is layered defense (the Swiss-cheese idea, C17, mental models, section 9). A <strong>guardrail</strong> is a control placed around a model to keep its behavior within limits: filters on inputs and outputs, rules about what it may do, limits on tools and permissions, and escalation to people. FW-C25-04 lays out the layers.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Scope and design.</strong> Limit what the feature does (answer from documents, no medical advice), and design out the dangerous capabilities (no money movement without approval).</li>\n<li><strong>Input defenses.</strong> Detect and handle prompt injection, abusive and out-of-scope requests; limit the rate per user (<strong>rate limiting</strong>, C22.4).</li>\n<li><strong>Model behavior.</strong> Instructions, training and refusal behavior that decline harmful requests, tested for over-refusal as well as under-refusal (a safety filter is a classifier with precision and recall, C23.3).</li>\n<li><strong>Output defenses.</strong> Filters for harmful or private content, grounding checks (is each claim supported?), format constraints.</li>\n<li><strong>Permissions and actions.</strong> Least privilege, approvals, reversibility, logging (C24.4).</li>\n<li><strong>Human review and escalation.</strong> Queues for flagged outputs, a path for users to report, and authority to restrict behaviors quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring and response.</strong> Sample live traffic, track harm rates and drift, run incident response with a kill switch and a postmortem (C17.5).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Red-teaming.</strong> <strong>Red-teaming</strong> is deliberate adversarial testing in which people try to make a system fail or cause harm, to find weaknesses before real users do. Use internal and external testers, a harm taxonomy (dangerous advice, harassment, sexual content, privacy, deception, manipulation, security), and track the attack success rate per category. New attack methods appear continually, so red-teaming is continuous work.</p>\n<p><strong>Layer arithmetic.</strong> If an input filter stops 80% of attack attempts, the model refuses 90% of those that pass, and an output filter catches 70% of what remains, then the share of attempts that succeed is 0.2 × 0.1 × 0.3 = 0.6%. At 100,000 attempts a day that is 600 successful attacks; improving the output filter to 90% cuts that to 200, and limiting repeat attackers to halve the attempts cuts it to 100 (Worked example 4). Each layer is imperfect, and their product is much better than any one.</p>\n<p><strong>Drift.</strong> <strong>Drift</strong> is the change over time in inputs, user behavior or the world that makes a deployed model perform worse than when it was evaluated. Detect it by monitoring input distributions, the share of outputs flagged, the eval score on fresh live samples and user signals; refresh the eval and retrain or re-prompt as needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Designing for failure.</strong> Assume the model will sometimes fail and plan the user's path: a fallback that shows the original content, a handoff to a person, a clear message, and a way to report. Practice incident response before launch: who can turn the feature off, how fast, and who tells users.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-c25-5-autonomy-and-trust-progression-for-agentic-products\"></span>\n<h4>C25.5 Autonomy and trust progression for agentic products</h4>\n<p>C24.4 introduced the autonomy ladder (suggest, draft, act with approval, act autonomously). Here is how to move along it with evidence (FW-C25-05).</p>\n<p><strong>Earned autonomy.</strong> Start each capability at the lowest level that is useful. Promote it only when (1) the error rate at the current level is measured on enough cases and below a bar set from the cost of error; (2) the action is reversible or capped in amount; (3) monitoring and a kill switch exist; and (4) users' overrides are low. Demote on a rule written in advance: if errors exceed a threshold in a week, or one severe incident occurs, drop a level and investigate.</p>\n<p><strong>How much evidence?</strong> To claim that an error rate is below 1% with 95% confidence after observing no errors, you need about 3 ÷ 0.01 = 300 supervised cases (the rule of three <code>[V: arithmetic checked]</code>); if you want to claim below 0.2%, about 1,500. If errors occur, you need more. Evidence should be gathered per category of action and per amount band, since reliability on small refunds says little about large ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Limits that make autonomy safe.</strong> Caps on value (refunds under $20), counts (ten actions an hour), scope (only the customer in the conversation), time (revoke after a session) and audit trails (every action logged with the reason and the evidence). Autonomy without limits is a bet on the model's behavior; limits turn it into a bounded risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Trust is two-sided.</strong> Users' trust should track reliability: tell people what the agent has done, make actions easy to review and undo, and avoid presenting confident prose over uncertain actions.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-c25-6-economics-cost-per-task-pricing-margins\"></span>\n<h4>C25.6 Economics: cost per task, pricing, margins</h4>\n<p>AI features have a real, variable cost per use (C24.7) that changes how products are priced and how margins behave (FW-C25-06; C18.6 gave the general method).</p>\n<p><strong>Cost per task.</strong> Cost per task = tokens in × input price + tokens out × output price, plus retrieval, tools, human review and support. Track it per task type and per user, not on average.</p>\n<p><strong>Heavy users.</strong> Usage is uneven. Suppose a $20 per-seat monthly add-on: an average user runs 300 tasks at $0.03 each, $9, plus $2 of other cost: a $9 margin (45%). A heavy user running 1,500 tasks costs $45 plus $2: a loss of $27. If 10% of users are heavy, the blended cost is 0.9 × $11 + 0.1 × $47 = $14.60 and the margin falls to $5.40 (27%) (Worked example 5). Flat pricing with unlimited use exposes the product to its heaviest users.</p>\n<p><strong>Levers.</strong> Route easy tasks to cheaper models (if 60% of tasks cost a tenth as much, the average task cost falls from $0.03 to $0.0138 and the blended margin rises to about $12.20, 61%); cache; shorten prompts; limit outputs; set usage tiers or included allowances with metered overage; price by outcome where value is measurable (a resolved ticket); and design features that reduce unnecessary calls.</p>\n<p><strong>Pricing models.</strong> Per seat (simple, predictable, exposed to heavy users), per usage (aligns cost and price, unpredictable for customers), per outcome (aligns with value, needs measurable outcomes and trust in measurement), and bundling into an existing product (hides the cost and must be covered by the product's margin) <code>[V: All four patterns exist in vendor pricing (2025 to 2026 reports): per seat (Microsoft 365 Copilot), per usage or conversation (Salesforce Agentforce, credit-based plans), per outcome (Intercom's Fin at about 0.99 dollars per outcome, and Zendesk per automated resolution; Intercom renamed itself Fin in 2026, agreed on 15 June 2026 to be acquired by Salesforce, and Salesforce announced the completion on 10 September 2026, per Salesforce's releases), bundling into suites (Microsoft); prices change often and are not quoted in the book]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not assume costs fall on their own.</strong> Prices for the same capability have tended to decline over time <code>[V: Stanford HAI, AI Index 2025 (with Epoch AI data): GPT-3.5-level performance fell from 20 to 0.07 dollars per million tokens, November 2022 to October 2024; falls of 9 to 900 times per year by task; a fixed-capability price, not frontier-model pricing]</code>, and usage and expectations rise with them; plan margins on current costs and treat decline as upside.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-c25-7-regulation-and-compliance-overview-by-region\"></span>\n<h4>C25.7 Regulation and compliance overview by region</h4>\n<p><code>[as of 2026-10]</code> This section is an overview for orientation, not legal advice; involve counsel and check current texts, because rules and dates change quickly. The practical approach (FW-C25-07) is to classify each AI use by risk and apply the controls that tier requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk tiers.</strong> A <strong>risk tier</strong> is a category of AI use ranked by the potential for harm, which sets the obligations on its provider and deployer (as in the risk-based approach of the European Union's AI Act). A typical ordering: <em>prohibited</em> uses; <em>high-risk</em> uses (for example employment decisions, credit, education, essential services, safety components); <em>limited-risk</em> uses that require transparency (chatbots must disclose that the user is talking to a machine, AI-generated content may need labeling); and <em>minimal-risk</em> uses (a spam filter) with no special obligations.</p>\n<p><strong>Regions (as of 2026-10, check each).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>European Union.</em> The AI Act entered into force in August 2024 and applies in phases: prohibited practices since February 2025; obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models since August 2025; transparency obligations for chatbots and generated content (Article 50) from 2 August 2026, with a transition to 2 December 2026 for machine-readable marking by generative systems already on the market; and the high-risk obligations, originally due 2 August 2026, deferred by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (the Digital Omnibus on AI, in force since 27 July 2026) to 2 December 2027 for stand-alone high-risk systems and 2 August 2028 for AI embedded in regulated products, with two new prohibitions (generating non-consensual intimate imagery and child sexual abuse material) from 2 December 2026 <code>[V: Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 on EUR-Lex (title, recital 38); legal summaries for the dates]</code>. Data protection law (the GDPR) also applies, including rules on solely automated decisions.</li>\n<li><em>United States.</em> There is no single comprehensive federal AI statute as of mid-2026 <code>[V: law-firm and industry summaries through July 2026; not rechecked for October 2026]</code>; Executive Order 14179 of January 2025 was followed by an executive order of 11 December 2025, &quot;Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence,&quot; which directs the Department of Justice to challenge state AI laws and does not itself preempt them <code>[V: law-firm and industry summaries, 2026]</code>; federal agencies apply existing laws (consumer protection against deceptive claims, anti-discrimination in employment and credit), and several states have passed or proposed AI laws, with dates that have shifted: Texas's Responsible AI Governance Act and California's frontier-model transparency law (SB 53) took effect on 1 January 2026, and Colorado, whose original AI Act had been postponed to 30 June 2026, repealed and replaced it on 14 May 2026 with a narrower law on automated decision-making technology that takes effect on 1 January 2027 <code>[V: law-firm alerts, May to July 2026]</code>. Some cities regulate specific uses, such as New York City's Local Law 144, enforced from 5 July 2023, which requires annual independent bias audits of automated employment tools <code>[V: NYC Local Law 144: annual independent bias audit, published summary, notice; enforced from 5 July 2023]</code>.</li>\n<li><em>India.</em> The Digital Personal Data Protection Act of 2023, with rules notified on 13 November 2025, with the Data Protection Board provisions effective at once, consent-manager provisions from 13 November 2026 and the main obligations from 13 May 2027, governs personal data <code>[V: law-firm summaries of the DPDP Rules]</code>; India has no standalone AI statute, the Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology issued non-binding AI Governance Guidelines on 5 November 2025, and amendments to the IT Rules effective 20 February 2026 require platforms to label and trace synthetically generated content and shorten takedown times <code>[V: law-firm summaries; Hogan Lovells, 2026]</code>.</li>\n<li><em>China.</em> Rules apply to recommendation algorithms, synthetic media and generative AI services, with requirements on content and registration <code>[V: China: algorithm recommendation provisions (effective 1 March 2022), deep synthesis provisions (10 January 2023), generative AI interim measures (15 August 2023); filing and security assessment duties]</code>. Two further instruments followed: labeling measures for AI-generated content, effective 1 September 2025 with a mandatory national standard, and interim measures for anthropomorphic (companion-style) AI interaction services, issued on 10 April 2026 and effective 15 July 2026 <code>[V: law-firm and legal-directory summaries, 2025 to 2026]</code>.</li>\n<li><em>Elsewhere.</em> The United Kingdom follows a non-statutory, principles-based approach through existing regulators, with no standalone AI statute as of mid-2026, and others take similar routes <code>[V: UK: March 2023 white paper, five non-statutory principles applied by existing regulators; no AI statute as of mid-2026 (RPC, 1 June 2026)]</code>; the May 2026 King's Speech announced a Regulating for Growth Bill with cross-economy sandbox powers and not a dedicated AI Act, and a law-firm note of 14 September 2026 says a dedicated AI bill had still not appeared <code>[V: RPC; Lewis Silkin, 14 September 2026]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What a PM does.</strong> Classify the use by tier and region; keep documentation (what the system is for, its data, its evaluation results, known limits, and monitoring), often called a <strong>model card</strong> when it describes a model: a short document stating a model's intended use, data, evaluation results, limitations and risks; build in required transparency (disclose AI interaction, label generated content, offer human review for significant decisions); keep records for audit; and bring in legal and policy early. A feature that is high-risk in one market may need a different design or a delayed launch there.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-c25-8-defensibility\"></span>\n<h4>C25.8 Defensibility</h4>\n<p>If models are available to everyone, what makes an AI product hard to copy? Use the sources-of-advantage test (FW-C18-05) with an AI lens (<code>Inferred</code>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Proprietary data and feedback loops:</em> data that others cannot get and that improves the product with use (but beware that general models may learn the task without your data).</li>\n<li><em>Distribution:</em> access to users through an existing product or channel.</li>\n<li><em>Workflow integration and switching costs:</em> the product sits inside how people work, with their data, templates and habits.</li>\n<li><em>Evaluation capability:</em> a mature test set and monitoring let you improve faster and ship safely; competitors starting from nothing are slower.</li>\n<li><em>Trust and brand:</em> in domains where errors are costly, a track record of reliability and accountability.</li>\n<li><em>Cost position:</em> routing, caching and fine-tuning that deliver the same quality at lower cost.</li>\n<li><em>Weak sources:</em> a clever prompt (easy to copy), the choice of model (others can switch), and a thin wrapper around a general model.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A defensible answer says what compounds (data, workflow, trust) and what does not, and what you would build toward as models improve.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-c25-9-answer-architecture-for-ai-product-questions\"></span>\n<h4>C25.9 Answer architecture for AI product questions</h4>\n<p>Section 4 gives the full architecture. In outline: define the user problem and the cost of error; design the evaluation before the feature; choose metrics across layers and watch for acceptance traps; design safety in layers and test adversarially; set autonomy with evidence; compute cost per task and margins; note the risk tier and regulation; and say what is defensible.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-c25-10-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h4>C25.10 Worked examples</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives five worked examples: an eval design with sample size and rater agreement, a model-judge calibration, an online experiment with the acceptance trap, layered safety arithmetic, and unit economics for an AI add-on. Section 11 adds ten questions with model answers and follow-ups.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. The AI fit test (FW-C24-01), the cost and latency sheet (FW-C24-02), the AI failure-mode map (FW-C24-03), the autonomy ladder (FW-C24-05), the AI UX checklist (FW-C24-06), the experiment plan for ranking (FW-C23-07), the sources-of-advantage test (FW-C18-05) and the unit-economics sheet (FW-C18-06) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c25-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C25-01 · The eval design card · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an AI feature and the cost of its errors.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the task and failure types; cases by slice (including adversarial and unanswerable); the rubric with scored examples; judges (people, rules, calibrated model); a baseline; a pass bar with per-slice minimums; automation on every change; and production monitoring that feeds a &quot;never again&quot; set.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> real or realistic inputs can be obtained.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a larger, better-labeled set costs more and makes decisions safer.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a few clean demos; one overall number; no hidden test set; no regression run.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1 and CS-C25-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Task and failure types</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cases and slices</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rubric (criteria, scale, examples)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Judges</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Baseline and pass bar</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Run on every change; monitoring</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c25-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C25-02 · Eval sample size and uncertainty · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an expected error rate, a tolerable margin, the number of slices.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> cases needed per slice (n = 1.96² × p × (1 − p) ÷ δ²), the total, and the interval to report with each result; a note on zero-error claims (upper bound about 3 ÷ n).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> cases are a random sample of the use.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> tighter margins and more slices need many more cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> certifying a rate from 30 cases; reading a slice with five cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1 and EX-C25-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c25-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C25-03 · The AI metric stack · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an AI feature and its goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> metrics at five layers (output quality, product behavior, business, cost and speed, safety and trust), the primary metric, guardrails with margins, and the &quot;accepted and wrong&quot; measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> outputs can be sampled and judged in production.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> deeper measures are slower and costlier.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> acceptance as quality; no cost guardrail; no rare-harm sampling.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3 and IQ-C25-06.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c25-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C25-04 · Safety layers and the red-team plan · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an AI feature and its harm taxonomy.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the seven layers (scope, inputs, model behavior, outputs, permissions, human review, monitoring and response) with the share each stops; red-team coverage by harm category with attack success rates; the compounded leakage; incident roles and a kill switch.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> layer effectiveness can be measured by testing.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> each layer adds latency, cost and refusals of legitimate requests.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> one filter; testing only friendly cases; no kill switch; measuring only over-refusal or only under-refusal.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4 and CS-C25-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formula: <strong>leakage = product of the shares each layer lets through.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c25-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C25-05 · The trust progression ladder · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a capability, its autonomy levels and the cost of error.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> per capability and band: the current level, the evidence required to promote (cases, error bar, reversibility, monitoring), demotion triggers, limits (value, count, scope, time) and audit trail.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> errors can be detected and attributed.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> slower promotion is safer and keeps benefits away longer.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> promotion by demo; one level for all amounts; no demotion rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C25-08 and EX-C25-08.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c25-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C25-06 · The AI unit-economics sheet · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> price, tasks per user by segment, cost per task, other costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> margin by user segment, the blended margin, the heavy-user loss, levers (routing, caching, tiers, caps, outcome pricing) with their effect, and the break-even usage.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> usage by segment is measured, not guessed.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> caps protect margin and annoy heavy users.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> an average user only; flat unlimited pricing; assuming costs will fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5 and IQ-C25-07.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c25-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C25-07 · Compliance triage by risk tier · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an AI use, the markets served and the data involved.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the risk tier per market, the obligations that follow (transparency, documentation, human oversight, data protection), the documents to keep (model card, evaluation results, monitoring), the owners (legal, policy, security) and the launch implications.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the current text of each rule is checked <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> compliance adds work and gates; late discovery costs more.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating a deferral as an exemption; one global design for different markets; no documentation.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> IQ-C25-09 and EX-C25-09.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Market</th>\n<th>Tier</th>\n<th>Obligations</th>\n<th>Owner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c25-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C25-08 · The AI product answer spine · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an AI product question and 30 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer in seven steps: user problem and cost of error; eval design; metrics and experiment; safety layers and red-team; autonomy and trust progression; economics; regulation and defensibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can ask clarifying questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure shows judgment and can crowd out depth.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> building before evaluating; one metric; no safety plan; ignoring cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c25-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c25-the-ai-product-answer-for-a-30-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The AI product answer, for a 30-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>User problem and cost of error</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The job, the fit, what an error costs and who can catch it, the quality bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation design</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The test set (real, sliced, adversarial), the rubric, judges (people first), the pass bar, regression on every change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics and experiment</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The metric stack, the acceptance trap, shadow mode and a staged online test with a holdout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safety and autonomy</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Layers, red-team, incident plan; autonomy ladder with promotion and demotion rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Economics</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Cost per task, margin by user type, levers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regulation and defensibility</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Risk tier and obligations; what compounds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Close</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Decision, first milestone, what would change the plan</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer names a specific issue (evaluation of one feature, an acceptance rate, pricing), spend the time there and use the structure briefly. If you run behind, keep the eval design, the top risk with its safeguard, and one number on cost or sample size.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> State the bar and how you will measure it: &quot;A target of fewer than 2% unsupported claims, measured on 750 real cases, read by language.&quot; Say what you would not trust: &quot;I wouldn't accept the acceptance rate as quality.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I'd build the eval first, gate launch on [pass bar] with per-slice minimums, run shadow mode and then a staged test with a holdout, and add [safeguards] with a kill switch. I'd start autonomy at [level], promote on evidence, and revisit pricing and compliance at [milestones]; I'd change the plan if [signal].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c25-type-evaluate-an-ai-feature\"></span>\n<h4>Type: evaluate an AI feature</h4>\n<p>Eval design card, sample size, rubric, judges, regression, monitoring.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-type-an-ai-metric-looks-good-acceptance-engagement\"></span>\n<h4>Type: an AI metric looks good (acceptance, engagement)</h4>\n<p>The metric stack, &quot;accepted and wrong,&quot; the break-even error rate, a holdout.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-type-can-we-trust-a-model-to-judge\"></span>\n<h4>Type: can we trust a model to judge?</h4>\n<p>Calibrate against humans on a sample; precision and recall of the judge; triage design.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-type-safety-and-abuse\"></span>\n<h4>Type: safety and abuse</h4>\n<p>Layers, red-team plan, leakage arithmetic, incident response.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-type-autonomy-for-an-agent\"></span>\n<h4>Type: autonomy for an agent</h4>\n<p>Trust progression with evidence, limits and demotion rules.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-type-pricing-or-margins-of-an-ai-product\"></span>\n<h4>Type: pricing or margins of an AI product</h4>\n<p>Unit-economics sheet by user segment, levers, pricing model.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-type-regulation-or-defensibility\"></span>\n<h4>Type: regulation or defensibility</h4>\n<p>Risk tier triage by market; the sources-of-advantage test with an AI lens.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How would you test it?&quot;</td>\n<td>Eval design</td>\n<td>Real sliced cases, rubric, judges, pass bar, regression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How many examples?&quot;</td>\n<td>Sample size</td>\n<td>Give the formula and an example; mention slices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Acceptance is 45%.&quot;</td>\n<td>Over-reliance</td>\n<td>Ask how many accepted outputs are wrong; compute break-even</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Can a model grade it?&quot;</td>\n<td>Judge validity</td>\n<td>Calibrate against humans; report precision and recall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What if it's attacked?&quot;</td>\n<td>Safety</td>\n<td>Layers, red-team, leakage, kill switch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;When does it act alone?&quot;</td>\n<td>Trust progression</td>\n<td>Evidence thresholds, limits and demotion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Does this make money?&quot;</td>\n<td>Economics</td>\n<td>Cost per task by segment and the heavy-user problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Is it regulated?&quot;</td>\n<td>Compliance</td>\n<td>Risk tier by market; checked dates; legal early</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c25-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional or use a product type as the setting only, and every figure is an invented assumption (invented), including every price and rate. The arithmetic was computed. Each is written as notes you could say aloud.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-worked-example-1-designing-the-eval-for-a-reply-drafting-ass\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Designing the eval for a reply-drafting assistant (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a drafting assistant inside an enterprise suite of the kind Microsoft offers, which writes replies to customer-support messages for an agent to edit and send. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Define the task and its failure types (FW-C25-01).</strong> The assistant drafts a reply from the customer's message and the company's help articles. Failures: (1) an <em>unsupported claim</em> (a refund promise or policy detail not in the articles); (2) a <em>missed urgent signal</em> (a threat to cancel, a safety issue); (3) <em>tone</em> that is wrong for the customer; (4) a <em>failure to decline</em> when the answer is not in the articles; (5) <em>leaking</em> another customer's details.</p>\n<p><strong>Collect cases.</strong> 500 real messages from the last quarter (invented), stratified by topic and by five languages, plus 100 adversarial cases (messages containing instructions to the assistant, requests for other customers' data, messages with no answer in the articles). A fifth of the cases is held out; engineers do not tune against it.</p>\n<p><strong>Size (FW-C25-02).</strong> To read the unsupported-claim rate, expected near 3%, within ±1.5 points at 95% confidence, the formula gives 1.96² × 0.03 × 0.97 ÷ 0.015² = about 497, so 500 cases. A single language with only 100 cases can only be read to about ±3.3 points (1.96 × √(0.03 × 0.97 ÷ 100)), enough to catch gross failure and not to certify a rate. So the plan: 500 overall for the headline, at least 100 per language as a floor with a &quot;no gross failure&quot; gate, and more cases in the languages with the most traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric and agreement.</strong> Three criteria scored in a few levels, each with examples: <em>supported</em> (every claim traceable to an article), <em>complete on urgency</em> (no urgent signal lost), <em>tone</em>. Two reviewers independently label 100 cases. Both flag 6 as unsupported, both pass 88, and they disagree on 6 (3 and 3). Raw agreement is 94%. But each reviewer flags about 9% of cases, so the agreement expected by chance is 0.09 × 0.09 + 0.91 × 0.91 = 83.6%, and the chance-corrected agreement (kappa) is (0.94 − 0.836) ÷ (1 − 0.836) = 0.63: moderate. The six disagreements are mostly about whether a paraphrase of an article counts as supported. The team adds scored examples of paraphrases to the rubric, and a second round of 100 gives 97 agreements; with reviewers flagging 9 and 10 cases, chance agreement is 82.8% and kappa rises to (0.97 − 0.828) ÷ 0.172 = 0.83.</p>\n<p><strong>Baseline and pass bar.</strong> Score the current approach and a simple one (retrieved article text pasted with no model): the simple one has near-zero unsupported claims and low usefulness. The pass bar, tied to the cost of an error (a wrong promise creates a refund and an unhappy customer, and agents catch most errors): at most 3% unsupported claims overall with the upper end of the 95% interval at most 4.5%; no missed urgent signal in the adversarial set; zero leaks; no language with a gross failure. Gate every change on it.</p>\n<p><strong>Run and monitor.</strong> The eval runs on every change to the model, prompt or retrieval and reports overall and per slice with intervals. In production, review 100 randomly sampled drafts a week, track agent edit and discard rates, and add every failure found to a &quot;never again&quot; set.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The eval was designed before the feature, sized with a formula, and made reliable by fixing the rubric when agreement was only moderate.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-worked-example-2-can-a-model-grade-the-outputs-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Can a model grade the outputs? (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> To avoid paying reviewers to read thousands of outputs, the team proposes using a language model as the grader. Before trusting it, they calibrate it against human judgments on 200 outputs (FW-C25-01).</p>\n<p><strong>The result.</strong> Humans judge 40 of the 200 outputs (invented) as bad (unsupported or wrong) and 160 as good. The model grader flags 28 outputs as bad: 24 of them are among the 40 humans found (true positives) and 4 are not (false positives). So it misses 16 of the 40 (false negatives), and correctly passes 156 of the 160 good ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Read it with precision and recall (C23.3).</strong> Accuracy: (24 + 156) ÷ 200 = 90%. Precision: 24 ÷ 28 = 86%: when it flags, it is usually right. Recall: 24 ÷ 40 = 60%: it finds only 60% of the real failures. The 90% agreement hides that two out of five bad outputs get through, because bad outputs are only 20% of the sample (a base rate effect, C12). If failures are rarer in production, the problem is worse.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the grader may miss them.</strong> Typical reasons: it favors fluent, long answers; it cannot verify facts it was not given; and its mistakes may be correlated with the system's own (both models make similar errors).</p>\n<p><strong>A design that uses it safely.</strong> Use the grader to triage: humans review everything it flags (28) plus a random 15% sample of what it passes (about 26 of the 172 passed). The expected review load is about 54 outputs instead of 200, a 73% reduction. The sample estimates what the grader misses: in the 172 it passed, 16 are bad (9.3%), so a 26-case sample would expect to find about 2.4 bad outputs; that estimate is noisy, so draw a larger sample each week and track the miss rate over time. Improve the grader (examples of subtle failures in its instructions, separate checks for each rubric criterion, a claim-by-claim check against the source) and recalibrate after each change.</p>\n<p><strong>What not to do.</strong> Do not use the grader alone to gate a launch, and do not use the same model to build and grade a feature without human calibration.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Agreement alone was misleading; recall on the failures that matter showed the grader's real limit, and a triage design captured most of the savings safely.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-worked-example-3-the-acceptance-rate-that-hides-errors-illus\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — The acceptance rate that hides errors (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: reply suggestions in a messaging product of the kind Meta operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A feature suggests a one-line reply. In an online test, 1,000,000 suggestions are shown and 450,000 are accepted: a 45% acceptance rate (invented). The team calls it a success.</p>\n<p><strong>Check what was accepted (FW-C25-03).</strong> Reviewers sample accepted suggestions and find that 8% contain a factual error or an inappropriate tone for the message: 0.08 × 450,000 = 36,000 accepted-with-error replies, 3.6% of all suggestions shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Value against cost.</strong> Each accepted suggestion saves about 22 seconds. Over 450,000 acceptances that is 2,750 hours. Suppose each erroneous reply costs about 10 minutes to the user in correction, confusion or a follow-up. Per 100 accepted suggestions: time saved 100 × 22 s = 36.7 minutes; errors 8 × 10 minutes = 80 minutes. The net is −43 minutes: the feature costs users more time than it saves. The break-even error rate is the time saved per acceptance divided by the cost per error: 22 s ÷ 600 s = 3.7%. At a 2% error rate the net is 36.7 − 20 = +16.7 minutes per 100 accepted. So the error rate among accepted suggestions, not the acceptance rate, decides whether the feature helps.</p>\n<p><strong>Read the time dimension (C14.4).</strong> Weekly acceptance falls from 52% to 45%, 42% and 40% as users learn which suggestions to trust; the first week was novelty. The long-run acceptance may settle near 38%.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Do not launch as is. Reduce the error rate (restrict suggestions to cases where the model is confident and where a wrong reply costs little; highlight names, dates and numbers for the user to check; never suggest replies on sensitive topics), re-measure accepted-with-error, and set a launch rule: accepted-with-error at most 2%, with an interval below the 3.7% break-even, complaints no higher than the control, and a holdout kept for 8 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A metric that looks like success can hide a net loss, and a simple break-even calculation turned it into a decision rule.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-worked-example-4-layers-red-teams-and-leakage-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Layers, red teams and leakage (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A consumer assistant will face adversarial users. The team designs layers and measures them (FW-C25-04). Suppose 100,000 attack attempts a day (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Red-team coverage.</strong> Eight harm categories (dangerous advice, self-harm, harassment, sexual content, privacy, deception and fraud, security, hate), 250 prompts each: 2,000 prompts. Before mitigations, 6% of attacks succeed overall; the most severe categories (self-harm, content involving minors) need the strictest bars.</p>\n<p><strong>Layer arithmetic.</strong> An input filter stops 80% of attempts, so 20% pass. The model's own refusal behavior stops 90% of those, so 10% pass. An output filter catches 70% of what remains, so 30% pass. The share that gets through is 0.2 × 0.1 × 0.3 = 0.6%. At 100,000 attempts a day that is 600 successful attacks a day. Improving the output filter to catch 90% reduces it to 0.2 × 0.1 × 0.1 = 0.2%: 200 a day. Rate limits and removal of repeat attackers that halve the attempts reduce it further: 50,000 × 0.2% = 100 a day. Each layer is imperfect, and their product is much better than any one.</p>\n<p><strong>What a clean red-team result proves.</strong> In the two most severe categories, 500 prompts each produced no successful attacks. That does not prove zero risk: with no failures in 500 trials the upper 95% bound on the failure rate is about 3 ÷ 500 = 0.6%, which would still be 600 per 100,000 attempts. So a clean test is a gate to launch, not an assurance; the remaining protection is monitoring and response.</p>\n<p><strong>Over-refusal.</strong> The input filter and the model also refuse legitimate requests. Measure the refusal rate on a set of benign but sensitive-sounding requests (a nurse asking about doses, a student asking about history), and tune filters on both errors (C23.3).</p>\n<p><strong>Response plan.</strong> Sample live conversations for review; a rapid-response owner with authority to restrict a behavior within hours; a per-category kill switch; a postmortem after any severe incident; and continuous red-teaming, with external testers, because attack methods change.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Compounding layers cut leakage by orders of magnitude, a clean test has an upper bound that must be said aloud, and the plan assumes some attacks will succeed.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-worked-example-5-will-an-ai-add-on-make-money-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — Will an AI add-on make money? (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A software company prices an AI assistant add-on at $20 per seat per month (invented). An average user runs 300 tasks a month at $0.03 each in model cost: $9, plus $2 of retrieval, support and other costs. A heavy user (10% of users) runs 1,500 tasks.</p>\n<p><strong>Margins by segment (FW-C25-06).</strong> Average user: cost $11, margin $9, 45%. Heavy user: tasks cost 1,500 × $0.03 = $45, plus $2: $47, margin −$27. Blended: 0.9 × $11 + 0.1 × $47 = $9.90 + $4.70 = $14.60, so the margin is $5.40, 27%. The average hides that a tenth of users generate a loss and that the loss eats more than half of the average user's margin.</p>\n<p><strong>Levers.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Route by difficulty.</em> Suppose 60% of tasks can be done by a model costing a tenth as much ($0.003): average cost per task is 0.4 × $0.03 + 0.6 × $0.003 = $0.0138. Average user: 300 × $0.0138 + $2 = $6.14; heavy user: 1,500 × $0.0138 + $2 = $22.70; blended: 0.9 × $6.14 + 0.1 × $22.70 = $7.80. Margin $12.20, 61%.</li>\n<li><em>Cap and meter.</em> Include 600 tasks a month and charge for overage, which makes heavy users pay for their use and protects the margin; test the effect on satisfaction and churn.</li>\n<li><em>Tiers.</em> A standard tier and a higher-priced tier for heavy use.</li>\n<li><em>Outcome pricing.</em> If the value is measurable (a support ticket resolved), price per outcome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What to monitor.</strong> Cost per task by task type and customer, the share of heavy users, margin by cohort, and the price elasticity of heavier tiers. Do not plan on falling model prices to save a bad margin; treat decline as upside <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The segment view exposed a heavy-user loss that the average hid, and routing and caps restored the margin with computed effects.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;How would you evaluate an AI assistant before launching it?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c25-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd have the team try it on a bunch of examples, ask some users for feedback in a beta and launch if people like it.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no test set, no rubric, no target, no count, no safety testing. &quot;People like it&quot; is not a measure of error.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd build an evaluation before the feature. I'd collect real questions, including the awkward ones and ones with no answer, write a rubric of what a good answer is, and have people score a sample, with two reviewers on part of it to check that they agree. I'd set a target tied to the cost of an error, such as fewer than 2% unsupported claims, and run the test on every change. I'd check results by language and topic, not only overall, and test adversarial inputs. Before launch I'd run it in shadow mode, then to a small group, and keep sampling live traffic.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c25-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd size the set: for a 3% error rate within 1.5 points I need about 500 cases, and each language I want to read separately needs its own sample, so I'd set a floor per language and a gross-failure gate. I'd check agreement beyond chance, since 94% raw agreement can be a kappa of 0.63 when most cases are good, and fix the rubric until it is reliable.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd use a model grader only after calibrating it: it might agree with humans 90% of the time and still catch only 60% of the failures. I'd use it to triage, with humans on what it flags and a sample of what it passes.</p>\n<p>&quot;For online testing I'd watch the metric that can fool me: acceptance. I'd measure accepted-and-wrong and compute a break-even: if a correct suggestion saves 22 seconds and a wrong one costs 10 minutes, the feature only helps below about a 3.7% error rate among accepted outputs.</p>\n<p>&quot;For safety I'd layer filters and model behavior and output checks, red-team by harm category, say what a clean test means (zero failures in 500 trials still allows 0.6%), and plan a kill switch and incident response. I'd start autonomy low and promote on evidence. I'd compute cost per task by user segment, since heavy users can turn a 45% margin into a loss, and check the risk tier and regional rules before launch. What would change my plan: a gap between eval and live performance, which tells me the test set no longer matches the use.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c25-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A test set, rubric, humans and a target</td>\n<td>Sizes the set, slices, checks agreement beyond chance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slices and adversarial cases; shadow mode</td>\n<td>Calibrates model graders; uses a break-even for acceptance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitors in production</td>\n<td>Layers and red-team with honest bounds; autonomy, cost and regulation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c25-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats and product facts change quickly <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google publishes AI principles and operates quality-rating processes for search <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google AI Principles (first published June 2018; updated 4 February 2025); Search Quality Rater Guidelines]</code>, and is described as publishing research on evaluating generated text (not rechecked here) <code>Inferred</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on evaluating generated answers and the validity of model graders <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: lead with a calibrated evaluation, slice by language and query type, and state what a model grader misses.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta has published model safety documentation and describes red-teaming of its models <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta Responsible AI page: red-teaming exercises, model cards, Responsible Use Guide, Llama Guard; the page described Llama 3.2]</code>, and it is described as doing the same for later models, including Muse Spark of April 2026 (not checked here) <code>Inferred</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on experiments for features at very large scale, including engagement versus quality <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: separate acceptance from correctness, read effects by week, and plan for rare harms that appear only at scale.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft has published a Responsible AI Standard and learned publicly from its 2016 Tay chatbot <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Responsible AI Standard (2022); Tay account read through excerpts and quoted passages]</code>, and is described as running an AI red team (not rechecked here) <code>Inferred</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on responsible AI, enterprise assistants and compliance <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: show a governance process (risk tiers, documentation, red-teaming, incident response) and enterprise controls.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon offers cloud services with configurable safeguards for generative AI and runs large-scale commerce and voice products <code>Public</code> <code>[V: AWS: Amazon Bedrock includes tools for safeguarding models and guardrails; Amazon runs commerce and voice products]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on cost per task, pricing and operational excellence <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: compute unit economics by segment, discuss cost levers and mechanisms for monitoring, and tie quality to customer outcomes.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple describes privacy as a design principle and has described on-device processing <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple privacy and support pages]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on evaluating features under privacy constraints, where raw user data cannot simply be collected <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: propose evaluation with synthetic or consented data, aggregated privacy-preserving measurement and a strong on-device fallback.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix is known for a strong experimentation culture <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix Tech Blog posts on experimentation and resilience testing]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts on rigorous testing and long-term effects <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: use holdouts, read effects over time, and tie AI feature value to retention, not clicks.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe's payments business involves automated decisions about risk and disputes with money at stake <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe pricing page and product descriptions (Radar machine learning)]</code>. Candidates report prompts on risk, thresholds and automation <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: design autonomy by amount and reversibility, with evidence thresholds, caps and audit trails.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c25-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Evaluating after building</td>\n<td>&quot;We'll test it later&quot;</td>\n<td>Build the eval first; it is the specification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A few clean demos</td>\n<td>Ten good examples</td>\n<td>Real, sliced, adversarial cases with a count</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>One overall number</td>\n<td>An average hides a failing slice</td>\n<td>Report per slice with intervals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Raw agreement as reliability</td>\n<td>&quot;94% agree&quot;</td>\n<td>Report chance-corrected agreement; fix the rubric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Trusting a model grader blindly</td>\n<td>A judge with no calibration</td>\n<td>Calibrate; measure its recall on real failures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Acceptance as quality</td>\n<td>A high acceptance rate</td>\n<td>Measure accepted-and-wrong; compute the break-even</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>One filter as safety</td>\n<td>A single moderation step</td>\n<td>Layers, red-team, kill switch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>A clean test read as zero risk</td>\n<td>&quot;No failures in 500 tests&quot;</td>\n<td>State the upper bound; monitor in production</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Autonomy by demo</td>\n<td>&quot;It worked in the demo&quot;</td>\n<td>Promote on evidence with limits and demotion rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Average-user economics</td>\n<td>One margin figure</td>\n<td>Margin by segment; plan for heavy users</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c25-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> is from C02 and <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Goodhart's law</strong> (<code>MM-goodhart</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C02). When a measure becomes a target it stops being a good measure. Acceptance rates, eval scores and engagement can all be gamed by the system or the team; pair each with a measure of what you actually want (accepted-and-wrong, a hidden test set, long-term retention).</p>\n<p><strong>The rule of three</strong> (<code>MM-rule-of-three</code>). If an event has not occurred in n independent trials, the 95% upper bound on its rate is about 3 ÷ n <code>[V: arithmetic checked]</code>. It tells you what a clean test proves: no failures in 500 tests means a rate that could still be 0.6%, and it sizes the evidence needed to promote autonomy (about 300 supervised cases for a 1% bound).</p>\n<p><strong>Swiss cheese</strong> (<code>MM-swiss-cheese</code>, C17, mental models, section 9). Layers of defense with holes stop most failures when the holes do not line up. Multiply the share each layer lets through, and add layers that fail differently.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). To make a system safe, ask how you would make it fail or cause harm, and design against that list. Red-teaming is inversion done by people.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ML engineering.</strong> They build the pipeline and the automated eval. Agree on the test set, the rubric and the pass bar together, a hidden set they do not tune against, regression runs on every change, and how failures from production are added. Ask for per-slice results and intervals.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Design shapes how outputs are checked and corrected; research produces the user judgments that feed rubrics and shows how people actually rely on suggestions. Test with real tasks, including the cases where the model is wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> They own the experiment design and the reading of metrics: effects by week, holdouts, power for rare harms, the accepted-and-wrong measure. Ask them to check that eval results predict live behavior.</li>\n<li><strong>Security, policy, legal and trust and safety.</strong> They define harm categories, run red teams, classify risk tiers and keep compliance records <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. Bring them in before the eval is final, so that the rubric reflects what the company must prevent.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c25-checklist-before-you-launch-an-ai-feature\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you launch an AI feature</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Is there an eval built before the feature, with real, sliced and adversarial cases, a rubric and a pass bar tied to the cost of error?</li>\n<li>Is the set large enough for the rate and the slices, and do I report intervals?</li>\n<li>Do reviewers agree beyond chance, and is any model grader calibrated against humans?</li>\n<li>Do my metrics separate output quality, product behavior, cost and safety, and do I measure accepted-and-wrong?</li>\n<li>Is there shadow mode, a staged launch and a long-term holdout?</li>\n<li>Are safety layers in place, red-teamed by harm category, with a kill switch and an incident owner?</li>\n<li>Is autonomy set by evidence with limits and demotion rules, and is cost per task known by user segment?</li>\n<li>Do I know the risk tier and the rules in each market, and the documentation to keep?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c25-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c25-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C25-01 · The prompt change that fixed the tone and broke refunds · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A support assistant answers customers using the help articles. Reviewers complain that its tone is stiff. An engineer revises the prompt to sound warmer. In a quick check on a few conversations, the new replies read better, and the change is ready to ship.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A prompt is easy to change and its effects are not local; the team has an evaluation suite of 400 cases from real conversations, 50 of them about refunds, but the check did not use it.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The team runs the suite before shipping. Before the change: 376 of 400 cases pass (94%): refunds 48 of 50 (96%) and all others 328 of 350 (93.7%). After the change: 364 of 400 pass (91%): refunds 39 of 50 (78%) and all others 325 of 350 (92.9%). The average tone score from reviewers rises by 0.4 on a five-point scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;A prompt change that improved tone lowered the refund category's pass rate from 96% to 78%, and the overall number moved only three points, so the quick check missed it.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> The change did what it was meant to do (tone improved) and broke something nobody was looking at: the warmer wording led the model to promise refunds more freely, which the suite marks as unsupported claims. The overall pass rate fell from 94% to 91%, which looks like a small drop; the refund slice fell by 18 points. Nine of the twelve lost passes came from the refund slice (48 to 39), because the other categories lost only three (328 to 325). This is the point of reading by slice (C25.1): an average can hold nearly steady while one category collapses, and the category that collapsed is the one with money attached. The regression suite exists to catch exactly this, and it only helps if it is run on every change.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Ship the warmer prompt; fix refunds if complaints appear</td>\n<td>Speed and tone</td>\n<td>Wrong refund promises reach customers; cost and trust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Revise the prompt to keep the warmer tone and add explicit refund instructions, then rerun the suite with a per-category gate</td>\n<td>Tone and accuracy</td>\n<td>An hour or two of work; a gate that can block small changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Revert the change</td>\n<td>Safety</td>\n<td>Loses the tone improvement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs a little time and keeps both gains; A risks real money; C wastes the improvement.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B. Add a refund-specific instruction and examples, rerun the suite, and gate releases on a per-category rule: overall at least 93%, no category below 92%, and the refund category zero tolerance for unsupported promises in the adversarial set. Make the suite run automatically on every prompt or model change.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario the revised prompt scored 47 of 50 on refunds (94%), 93% overall and a tone gain of 0.35, and shipped. The suite caught two further regressions over the next quarter before release (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the revision had not recovered refunds, the team would have sent refund questions through a more constrained flow with a template and human review. If the suite had been too small to read the refund slice (five cases), the regression would not have been visible, which is why slices need their own cases. The lesson: run the regression suite on every change and read it by slice.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c25-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C25-02 · Microsoft's Tay, 2016: launched into an adversarial crowd · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): Microsoft's own account, &quot;Learning from Tay's introduction&quot; by Peter Lee, a corporate vice president (Official Microsoft Blog, 25 March 2016), the primary source for Microsoft's statements and what it said it had done before launch, read in full; reference and press summaries for the timeline, the number of tweets, the &quot;repeat after me&quot; behavior and the re-release of 30 March; and Microsoft's later documents (its Responsible AI Standard v2 of June 2022 and its security blog on its AI Red Team). The sources do not reveal Microsoft's internal test results. The analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> On Wednesday 23 March 2016 Microsoft launched Tay, a chatbot on Twitter and other messaging services designed to talk with 18- to 24-year-olds and to learn from its conversations. Microsoft had run a similar chatbot, XiaoIce, in China, which Microsoft's post said was being used by some 40 million people, and which press reports described as running without major incident <code>[V: Microsoft's post; press]</code>. Within hours users were prompting Tay to say offensive things, and it posted offensive and hurtful messages; coverage reported that many of its offensive tweets exploited a &quot;repeat after me&quot; capability, and it is not publicly known whether that capability was built in or learned <code>[V: reference summary of press reports]</code>. Microsoft deleted some tweets and suspended the account; reference summaries say this came within 16 hours of launch and after Tay had tweeted more than 96,000 times, while Microsoft's own post says the attack came in the first 24 hours of Tay coming online <code>[V: Microsoft's post; reference summaries]</code>. In its blog post two days after the launch, Microsoft apologized, said that Tay had been the target of a &quot;coordinated attack by a subset of people&quot; who exploited a vulnerability, noted that it had planned and implemented filtering and conducted user studies with diverse groups and had stress-tested Tay under a variety of conditions, and said it would bring Tay back only when it was confident it could better anticipate malicious intent. Reference summaries say Microsoft accidentally re-released Tay on Twitter on 30 March while testing it, after which it was taken offline again <code>[V: reference summaries]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> A system designed to learn from an open public audience in real time; a platform where a determined subset of users will probe for weaknesses; a launch to the public with no gradual exposure described in the sources; a capability (repeating user text) that provided a direct path to harmful output.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the dates; the design intent; the pre-launch filtering, user studies and stress tests; the speed and volume of the failure; Microsoft's statement of cause and its commitments. The test results and the monitoring set-up are not described.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> A conversational system was exposed to a public that included adversaries, and its protections did not hold against deliberate manipulation. As an observable problem: &quot;Within hours of launch, users caused the system to produce offensive content at scale, and it had to be taken offline.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, through this chapter's tools.</em></p>\n<p><em>Adversarial testing (C25.4).</em> Microsoft said it stress-tested Tay under many conditions with diverse users. Friendly diverse testers are not adversaries. The failure came from a coordinated, deliberate attack, which a red team exists to simulate: people whose job is to find the &quot;repeat after me&quot; path and the coordinated-flood approach before launch. The sources do not say whether such testing was done, so this is a reading of the gap, not a finding.</p>\n<p><em>Layers (FW-C25-04).</em> A single layer of filtering faced an adversarial crowd. Output filters, limits on what could be repeated or learned, rate limits on any one user's influence, and a safeguard against learning from unvetted input would multiply: if each stopped 70% to 90% of attempts, the product of several layers leaks a small fraction; one filter leaks far more.</p>\n<p><em>Learning from an open stream.</em> A system that learns from user input is a feedback loop (C23.8) that attackers can steer. Learning should be gated: what the system adapts from, how fast, and with what review.</p>\n<p><em>Staged exposure and the kill switch (C17.2, C17.5).</em> The sources suggest a broad public release at once. A smaller initial audience, a controlled invitation list and per-category monitoring would have limited the blast radius. The speed of Microsoft's shutdown (reported as about 16 hours) shows that a kill switch existed and worked, but the volume (tens of thousands of messages) shows how quickly harm accumulates without earlier detection; rate limits and automatic pauses on spikes in flagged content act faster than people.</p>\n<p><em>Accountability.</em> Microsoft's post accepted responsibility for the outcome and named what it would change, which is the posture of an incident review (a postmortem, C17.5). The accidental re-release a few days later, if accurate, illustrates the operational risk of controls that people can switch off by mistake.</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> The content of the stress tests, the internal monitoring, the exact mechanism of the attack beyond the &quot;repeat after me&quot; description, and what changed afterward. The case supports an analysis of adversarial testing, layering and staged release, not a judgment about the individuals involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a team could face; not a record of Microsoft's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Public launch with filtering and friendly testing, as described</td>\n<td>Speed and learning from real users</td>\n<td>Exposure to organized adversaries; rapid harm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Staged launch (small invited group first) with a red team, rate limits, gated learning and automatic pauses</td>\n<td>Safety and learning</td>\n<td>Slower growth; more engineering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Launch without learning from users; fixed behavior with scripted limits</td>\n<td>Predictability</td>\n<td>Less engaging; less learning</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B keeps most of the benefit and controls the blast radius; A is fastest and carries the tail risk that occurred; C is safest and loses the purpose of the experiment.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, Microsoft launched publicly, took Tay offline within about a day, apologized and said it would learn from the experience <code>[V: Microsoft's post]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: Tay was offline within about a day (reported as 16 hours) and became a widely cited cautionary example; Microsoft later published six AI principles (in 2018), a Responsible AI Standard (version 2, June 2022), and its AI Red Team began in 2018 <code>[V: Microsoft documents]</code>. Direct causation from Tay to those later practices is not established in the sources used here.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> A staged release with a red team might have found the repeat path before launch and limited the audience when the first spike appeared. A reader should avoid hindsight: the 2016 field had few established practices for adversarial testing of conversational systems. The method to carry away: test with adversaries as well as friendly users, layer defenses, gate what the system learns from, expose it gradually, and make sure that the people who can switch it off can do so within minutes.</p>\n<span id=\"c25-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Ten questions. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), every figure is an assumption, and the arithmetic was computed. Each ends with follow-ups and answers. Where a real company's product is the setting, the answer reasons from public information and states no internal fact. Product facts, prices and regulations in this field change quickly <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. The answers are fuller than a first pass in a real round.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-01 · Beginner · ai · AI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is an eval, and why would you build it before the feature?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's a test of the AI.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes a test set, a rubric and a target, and says it guides building.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains the eval as the specification, covers real and adversarial cases, slices, human and model judges, regression runs on every change, and the hidden test set.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> An eval is a repeatable test: a set of cases, a way to score the outputs, and a target. For an AI feature it is the specification, because I cannot write &quot;the answer shall be correct&quot; as a rule; I can only say what good looks like on concrete cases.</p>\n<p>I build it first for three reasons. It decides what I build: writing down the failures that matter (an invented policy, a missed urgent message, a leak of another person's data) shapes the design. It lets me compare approaches fairly: prompt against retrieval, one model against another, scored on the same cases. And it makes change safe: every prompt, model or data change reruns the eval, so a fix in one place does not quietly break another.</p>\n<p>What goes in it: real inputs from the intended use, including the awkward ones, other languages, adversarial inputs and questions with no answer; a short rubric with scored examples; judges (people first, rules where checkable, a calibrated model grader to extend reach); a pass bar tied to the cost of an error; and per-slice results, because an average can hide a failing category. I keep part of the set hidden from the engineers who tune the system, and I add every production failure to a &quot;never again&quot; set.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Isn't this slow? We could ship and learn from users. <strong>A.</strong> &quot;For a low-stakes reversible feature, a small eval (50 to 100 real cases) takes days and avoids shipping blind; for a costly error it is mandatory. Learning from users is still part of the plan (shadow mode, staged launch, monitoring), and the eval makes that learning faster because you know what changed.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you have no real inputs yet? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Start with the most realistic ones available: logs from the process the feature replaces, interviews, colleagues role-playing, or carefully written synthetic cases, marked as synthetic. Replace them with real traffic in shadow mode as soon as possible, and be cautious about scores on synthetic data.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-02 · Beginner · ai · AI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How many examples do you need in an evaluation set?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A few hundred should be fine.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Links the number to the precision needed and the number of slices.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives and applies the formula, notes that small sets cannot certify a low error rate, explains the rule of three, and plans per-slice samples and a floor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> It depends on how precisely I need to know an error rate and how many groups I want to read separately. To estimate a rate p within ±δ at 95% confidence, I need about n = 1.96² × p × (1 − p) ÷ δ² cases. For an expected error of 2% within ±1 point, n is about 753. For 5% within ±2 points, about 456. For 3% within ±1.5 points, about 497.</p>\n<p><em>Slices multiply the need.</em> If I want to read ten languages separately at that precision, I need about ten times as many cases: about 7,500 for the 2% example. When that is too expensive, I set a smaller floor per slice (100 cases reads a 3% rate only to about ±3.3 points) and use it as a gross-failure gate, and put more cases where traffic and risk are highest.</p>\n<p><em>Small sets and zero errors.</em> Thirty cases with no errors do not show that the error rate is low; the 95% upper bound is still about 3 ÷ 30 = 10% (the rule of three). To claim a rate below 1% from a clean run needs about 300 cases.</p>\n<p><em>Quality matters more than count.</em> Five hundred cases from the real distribution beat 5,000 cases that are all easy. Check that the set represents real use, including rare, costly situations, and refresh it.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the failures I care about are very rare, say 1 in 10,000? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A random sample will almost never contain one. Oversample candidate failures (cases flagged by filters, user reports, adversarial constructions), measure on those, and estimate the true rate by combining the flagged rate with the sampling fraction; and use live sampling with large volume and monitoring.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How often do you refresh the set? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Whenever the inputs or the product change and on a schedule (monthly or quarterly) from fresh live samples; compare scores on old and new sets, since a gap means the set has drifted from use.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c25-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C25-03 · Hard · ai · AI · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you evaluate the quality and safety of on-device summarization of notifications?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Test it with some users and see if they like it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines quality and safety criteria, builds a test set, and notes the privacy limit on data.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Handles the constraint that raw user notifications cannot be collected, sizes samples by language, covers sensitive categories and urgent signals, uses calibrated model graders and privacy-preserving measurement, and plans shadow mode, rollout and monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real feature or process <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>. The central difficulty is privacy: the point of the feature is that notifications stay on the device, so I cannot simply collect users' real notifications to test it. The eval design has to work around that.</p>\n<p><em>What good means (FW-C25-01).</em> Failures: an unsupported claim (a summary says something no notification said), a dropped urgent signal (&quot;call me now&quot; lost), a wrong sender or attribution, a summary of something that should never be summarized (a verification code, a medical or emergency alert), and poor quality in some languages.</p>\n<p><em>Where the cases come from.</em> (1) Synthetic notifications written to cover app types, languages and styles, and labeled as synthetic. (2) Notifications donated by consenting testers (employees, paid volunteers) under clear terms. (3) Public or licensed message corpora where appropriate. (4) Aggregated, privacy-preserving on-device measurement after launch, such as counts of &quot;show original&quot; taps, opt-outs and a rate of locally computed quality checks reported without content <code>[unverified: author's design suggestion; no source checked]</code>. Keep the cases diverse: short and long, multi-sender, slang, mixed languages, urgent and trivial.</p>\n<p><em>Sizing.</em> To read a 2% unsupported-claim rate within ±1 point in one language needs about 753 cases (1.96² × 0.02 × 0.98 ÷ 0.01²); for ten languages, about 7,530 summaries. At two reviewers each at $0.40 a label, labeling costs 7,530 × 2 × $0.40 = about $6,000, small relative to the risk. For lower-traffic languages use a smaller floor and restrict launch to languages that pass.</p>\n";
